ter in ClC chloride channels. <source>Science.</source> <volume>300</volume>:<fpage>108</fpage>&#x02013;112.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12649487</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Engh, A.M., and M. Maduke. <year>2005</year>. Cysteine accessibility in ClC-0 supports conservation of the ClC intracellular vestibule. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>125</volume>:<fpage>601</fpage>&#x02013;617.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15897295</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Engh, A.M., J. Faraldo-G&#x000f3;mez, and M. Maduke. <year>2007</year>. The mechanism of fast-gate opening in ClC-0. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>130</volume>:<fpage>335</fpage>&#x02013;349.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17846164</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Estevez, R., B.C. Schroeder, A. Accardi, T.J. Jentsch, and M. Pusch. <year>2003</year>. Conservation of chloride channel structure revealed by an inhibitor binding site in ClC-1. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>38</volume>:<fpage>47</fpage>&#x02013;59.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12691663</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Faraldo-G&#x000f3;mez, J.D., and B. Roux. <year>2004</year>. Electrostatics of ion stabilization in a ClC chloride channel homologue from <italic>Escherichia coli.</italic> <source>J. Mol. Biol.</source> <volume>339</volume>:<fpage>981</fpage>&#x02013;1000.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15165864</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Gervasio, F.L., M. Parnello, M. Ceccarellil, and M.L. Klein. <year>2006</year>. Exploring the gating mechanism in the ClC chloride channel via metadynamics. <source>J. Mol. Biol.</source> <volume>361</volume>:<fpage>390</fpage>&#x02013;398.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16843488</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Lin, C.W., and T.Y. Chen. <year>2000</year>. Cysteine modification of a putative pore residue in ClC-0:implication for the pore stoichiometry of ClC chloride channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>116</volume>:<fpage>535</fpage>&#x02013;546.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11004203</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Lin, C.W., and T.-Y. Chen. <year>2003</year>. Probing the pore of ClC-0 by substituted cysteine accessibility method using methane thiosulfonate reagents. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>122</volume>:<fpage>147</fpage>&#x02013;159.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12885876</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Lin, Y.-W., C.-W. Lin, and T.-Y. Chen. <year>1999</year>. Elimination of the slow gating of ClC-0 chloride channel by a point mutation. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>114</volume>:<fpage>1</fpage>&#x02013;12.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10398688</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Ludewig, U., M. Pusch, and T.J. Jentsch. <year>1996</year>. Two physically distinct pores in the dimeric ClC-0 chloride channel. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>383</volume>:<fpage>340</fpage>&#x02013;343.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8848047</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Ludewig, U., T.J. Jentsch, and M. Pusch. <year>1997</year>. Analysis of a protein region involved in permeation and gating of the voltage-gated <italic>Torpedo</italic> chloride channel ClC-0. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>498</volume>:<fpage>691</fpage>&#x02013;702.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9051580</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Maduke, M., C. Williams, and C. Miller. <year>1998</year>. Formation of CLC-0 chloride channels from separated transmembrane and cytoplasmic domains. <source>Biochemistry.</source> <volume>37</volume>:<fpage>1315</fpage>&#x02013;1321.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9477958</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Middleton, R.E., D.J. Pheasant, and C. Miller. <year>1996</year>. Homodimeric architecture of a ClC-type chloride ion channel. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>383</volume>:<fpage>337</fpage>&#x02013;383.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8848046</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Miloshevsky, G.V., and P.C. Jordan. <year>2004</year>. Anion pathway and potential energy profiles along curvilinear bacterial ClC Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> pores: electrostatic effects of charged residues. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>86</volume>:<fpage>825</fpage>&#x02013;835.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14747318</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Pusch, M., U. Ludewig, A. Rehfeldt, and T.J. Jentsch. <year>1995</year>. Gating of the voltage-dependent chloride channel ClC-0 by the permeant ion. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>373</volume>:<fpage>527</fpage>&#x02013;531.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7845466</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Traverso, S., G. Zifarelli, R. Aiello, and M. Pusch. <year>2006</year>. Proton sensing of ClC-0 mutant E166D. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>127</volume>:<fpage>51</fpage>&#x02013;65.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16380443</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Tsai, J., R. Taylor, C. Chothia, and M. Gerstein. <year>1999</year>. The packing density in proteins: standard radii and volumes. <source>J. Mol. Biol.</source> <volume>290</volume>:<fpage>253</fpage>&#x02013;266.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10388571</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Yin, J., Z. Kuang, U. Mahankali, and T.L. Beck. <year>2004</year>. Ion transit pathways and gating in ClC chloride channels. <source>Proteins.</source> <volume>57</volume>:<fpage>414</fpage>&#x02013;421.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15340928</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Zhang, X.D., Y. Li, W.P. Lu, and T.Y. Chen. <year>2006</year>. Roles of K149, G352, H401 in the channel functions of ClC-0: testing the predictions from theoretical calculations. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>127</volume>:<fpage>435</fpage>&#x02013;447.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16567465</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>